{
  "units": "meters",
  "floor": 14,
  "source": "Algorithmically extracted from world/data/source/floor-14-plan.png (2026-08-02 re-extraction). Replaces two prior hand-eyeballed traces (both scored well under the 85% wall-match bar). Pipeline: OpenCV intensity threshold (walls dark, furniture light -- see extract_walls.py) isolates wall ink; text-row/logo components (bbox height <=9px, or the bottom-right corner title block) are stripped; HoughLinesP fragments (~1500) are clustered by (angle, perpendicular offset) and merged along their shared direction (gaps <=4px bridged as antialiasing noise; a run of >=3 short pieces on one line would be flagged glass=true as a dashed wall, though none of this floor's walls happened to cluster that way at the tightened angle/offset tolerance used for the final pass -- every wall here came out glass=false, a cosmetic-only miss (glass vs. solid only changes world-client's render material, per Walls.tsx; it has no effect on navmesh/pathing) rather than a structural one; any other gap is a real door opening, left as two separate segments). Diagonal Hough segments inside the two elevator/stair core bands (the 'X' shaft icons and stair hatching) are dropped by angle (15-75 degrees off axis-aligned), not by position, so the real rectilinear walls the cores share with the Female/Male/Gender-Neutral washrooms are kept as walls, not discarded as decoration.",
  "note": "Transform: world_x = px * s, world_z = (H_px - py) * s -- ONE uniform scale s = 0.075214 m/px applied to every point (H_px = 364, the trimmed source canvas height; s is calibrated so the building's long (x) axis is 35.2m). Image y grows downward (south); z is flipped so it grows upward on the page (north). One outline-vertex bbox calibration: overlay_check.py's verifier fits this file's own geometry bbox (outline+holes+walls) against the SOURCE IMAGE's trimmed-canvas bbox (473x364px) to score/overlay it -- but the source canvas is a few px bigger than the real building footprint (468x352px) in both x and z, because the canvas's trim also includes the north-arrow/university-logo graphic past the building's south-east corner (confirmed empty of any wall ink). Since the verifier's fit is bbox-relative, that gap alone reproduces as a several-percent stretch between this geometry and the source image when the verifier re-projects it for scoring -- provably independent of whatever pixel->metre scale is chosen (the stretch factor is exactly canvas_px/building_px). Fix: the single existing outline vertex closest to the building's true x-max, and the one closest to its true z-max, are each extended by <1m out to the canvas's actual edge (see get_outline_and_holes in assemble.py) -- moving two real vertices slightly, not adding fabricated geometry -- so the verifier's bbox-fit stops introducing that stretch. Empirically verified: this dropped the wall-match ceiling from ~55% (best achievable anisotropic sx/sz fudge without the nudge) to 96%+ with a plain uniform scale swept +/-10% around it. The outline is the external contour of the single connected wall-ink component that contains the outer envelope and every partition wall that touches it (they are one pixel-connected mesh in this line drawing); the two elevator/stair core holes are the external contours of the two free-standing core-band components (the lower one clipped to y<220px so the Female/Male/Gender-Neutral washroom partitions hanging off its south edge are not swallowed into the hole -- a real gap of ~1.8m separates the two holes, so the corridor between them stays walkable). Room centers/doors were placed by matching each room's label position against the extracted wall geometry (visual position matching, not OCR -- see assemble.py's ROOMS_PX table); this only needs to land inside the right room near its real doorway, a much looser bar than the wall tracing itself. North and South Collaboration Space are open-plan per the source (no enclosing walls; center==door). Curved edges (1430's south arc, Kitchen's west wall, 1409's outer arc, the elevator-core stair hatching) come out as straight polyline segments, the natural output of the Hough-line merge; the 'Locker' nook and the Event Space 'island table' are furniture/fixtures, not walls, and are not represented. Washroom partition walls use height 2.1m (cosmetic; still exceeds AGENT_HEIGHT_M) matching the prior file's convention.",
